 ### [Specific Model Architectures and Extensions](10278-specific-model-architectures-and-extensions.md)
-This family encompasses the foundational neural radiance field architectures that map 6D input coordinates to density and color, serving as the baseline for subsequent optimizations and extensions. It solves the problem of implicit scene representation by learning a continuous volumetric function from multi-view images. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using a single, monolithic Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) without spatial partitioning or explicit geometric priors, relying entirely on positional encoding and hierarchical sampling to capture scene details. Unlike [Mip-NeRF 360](3306-mip-nerf-360.md) which handles unbounded scenes via contraction, or [NeRF-FF](8669-nerf-ff.md) which addresses defocus, the original architecture assumes a bounded, in-focus scene.
+This family represents a scene as a single MLP $f_\theta(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \to (\mathbf{c}, \sigma)$ that maps a 3D position $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and a viewing direction $\mathbf{d} \in S^2$ to an emitted color $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and a volumetric density $\sigma \ge 0$. Rendering uses the classical volume rendering integral along rays cast from the camera, with no explicit geometry—geometry emerges from where $\sigma$ is high after photometric optimization on multi-view images. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using a single, monolithic MLP without spatial partitioning or explicit geometric priors, relying entirely on positional encoding and hierarchical sampling to capture scene details. Unlike [Mip-NeRF 360](3306-mip-nerf-360.md) which handles unbounded scenes via contraction, or [NeRF-FF](8669-nerf-ff.md) which addresses defocus, the original architecture operates within defined near and far ray bounds.
 
