 ## Background
-The canonical NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. It established the paradigm of using a single MLP to map 6D coordinates to density and color. Subsequent developments addressed specific limitations: NeRFReN (2021) addressed reflective surfaces [S1]; Neural Body (2021) addressed dynamic human avatars using SMPL [S1, S2]; NeuMan (2021) addressed single-view human rendering [S1]; AR-NeRF (2021) addressed defocus [S1]; and SDF-based methods (e.g., VolSDF, 2021) addressed geometric extraction [S1]. The Nerfacto pipeline (2023) integrated many of these advances into a standard baseline [S3, unofficial].
+The canonical NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. It established the paradigm of using a single MLP to map 6D coordinates to radiance properties. Subsequent developments addressed specific limitations: reflective surfaces (NeRFReN, Neural Implicit Surfaces with PBR) [S1], dynamic humans (Neural Body, NeuMan) [S1, S2], defocus (AR-NeRF) [S1], and scalability (Scene Representation and Partitioning, Nerfacto) [S1][S3, unofficial]. The field has evolved from monolithic MLPs to hybrid representations and specialized architectures for specific domains like human avatars and reflective materials.
 
